\subsection{Contrastive Learning}
Contrastive learning is a type of self-supervised learning that aims to learn representations by contrasting positive and negative examples. The idea is to pull together positive examples (e.g., different views of the same image) and push apart negative examples (e.g., different images) in the representation space. This can be done by using a contrastive loss function, such as the InfoNCE loss \cite{arxivID-1807.03748-0}, which has been shown to be effective for learning representations from unlabeled data. 

